  "title": "HTTP/3 changes less than the demos imply",
  "content": "The obvious answer to a portfolio projects question in code review is usually the incomplete one. Here's what a colleague hitting this for the first time needs to know. The answers online were either wrong or skipped the part that mattered.\n\nHTTP/3 runs over QUIC, which runs over UDP. That one sentence is the whole reason backend teams end up with a migration ticket that sits open for months. The protocol is real, the browser support is real, and the CDN edge has supported it for years. What is not real, in most stacks, is a reason for the origin to care.\n\nThe problem this post solves is narrower than \"should we adopt HTTP/3\": it's the gap between what the edge terminates and what the origin actually sees. Most teams turn on HTTP/3 at the load balancer or CDN, watch the graph go green, and then discover that their application still behaves exactly as it did over HTTP/2 — same head-of-line blocking on the backend connection pool, same retry storms, same 502s when a single slow request pins a worker. The transport changed; the failure modes did not.\n\nThis is a walkthrough of where HTTP/3 and QUIC genuinely change backend behavior, where they are pure edge concerns, and how to reason about the boundary without a managed Kubernetes control plane or a dedicated network team.\n\n## The mechanism, briefly and precisely\n\nQUIC is a transport protocol built on UDP. It folds together what TCP, TLS 1.3, and a chunk of HTTP/2 stream multiplexing used to do in separate layers. The parts that matter for backend work:\n\n- **Streams are independent at the transport layer.** A lost packet on one stream does not block delivery on another, because QUIC tracks loss per stream rather than per connection. TCP has no concept of streams, so a lost segment stalls everything behind it at the kernel level — the head-of-line blocking that HTTP/2 could not fix because it sat above TCP.\n- **Connection establishment is shorter.** A first connection typically needs one round trip for the transport handshake plus the TLS handshake combined; repeat connections can use 0-RTT resumption. Over TCP+TLS 1.3 you are looking at more round trips before the first byte of application data.\n- **Connection migration is built in.** A QUIC connection is identified by a connection ID, not by the 4-tuple of source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port. A client switching from Wi-Fi to cellular can keep the connection alive. TCP cannot do this without help from a higher layer.\n- **Encryption is mandatory, not optional.** There is no cleartext QUIC in the deployed web. Header protection means middleboxes cannot inspect or rewrite transport headers the way they can with TCP.\n\nThat last point is the one that quietly breaks things. More on it below.\n\n## What actually changes at the origin\n\nIf your architecture is `client -> CDN/edge -> origin`, and the edge speaks HTTP/3 to clients while speaking HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 to your origin, then **nothing at your origin changes**. The edge terminates QUIC, reassembles streams, and forwards ordinary HTTP requests over a TCP connection. Your application does not know or care.\n\nThis is the common case, and it's why so many \"we enabled HTTP/3\" announcements are, from the backend's perspective, a no-op. The user-perceived latency improvement comes from the client-to-edge leg, where the round-trip savings are largest for high-latency, lossy mobile networks. The origin-to-edge leg is usually a short, well-provisioned path.\n\nHTTP/3 reaches the origin only when you explicitly configure it there: a load balancer or reverse proxy that terminates QUIC and forwards to your app, or (rarer) an application server that speaks QUIC directly. When that happens, the changes are concrete:\n\n| Concern | HTTP/2 over TCP | HTTP/3 over QUIC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Loss handling | One lost segment stalls the connection | Loss isolated to the affected stream |\n| Connection identity | 4-tuple; breaks on NAT rebind / IP change | Connection ID; survives migration |\n| Handshake | TCP + TLS, multiple RTTs | Combined, 1-RTT, 0-RTT on resume |\n| Middlebox visibility | Full transport header visibility | Headers encrypted; limited inspection |\n| CPU profile | Kernel TCP stack, often offloaded | Userspace crypto per packet, higher CPU |\n| Operational tooling | Mature: `ss`, `tcpdump`, conntrack | Newer; `qlog`, different capture workflow |\n\nThat CPU row is not a footnote. QUIC moves encryption and congestion control into userspace. On a busy edge, that is more CPU per byte than kernel TCP with hardware offload. Teams that terminate QUIC on general-purpose VMs commonly see the CPU cost show up before any latency benefit does.\n\n## Where the demos mislead\n\nThe typical HTTP/3 demo shows a page load on a simulated lossy mobile connection, with HTTP/3 winning on time-to-first-byte and full load time. That result is real and well documented. It is also not the workload most backend teams are optimizing.\n\nThree patterns where the demo does not transfer:\n\n**1. Server-to-server traffic on a stable network.** Two services in the same region, on a low-loss path, gain little from QUIC's loss isolation. The handshake savings matter for cold connections; a pooled, long-lived connection amortizes the handshake to nothing. The CPU cost of userspace crypto is paid on every byte.\n\n**2. Workloads dominated by a single large response.** Stream independence helps when many streams compete and one stalls. If a request is one big body on one stream, a lost packet stalls that stream regardless of transport. QUIC does not make a single stream faster than a single TCP connection on the same path.\n\n**3. Anything behind a middlebox that drops UDP.** This is the failure mode that produces the most confused incident reports. Corporate firewalls, some VPNs, and older network equipment block or rate-limit UDP/443. The client falls back to HTTP/2 — usually — but fallback is not guaranteed to be fast, and if you have disabled HTTP/2 at the edge because \"we're on HTTP/3 now,\" you have broken those users outright.\n\nThat third point is the one to internalize: **HTTP/3 is additive, never a replacement at the edge.** Keep HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 listeners. The `Alt-Svc` header is how a server advertises HTTP/3 availability so clients can attempt it and fall back cleanly.\n\n## The approach that works in practice\n\nA decision framework, in order. Stop at the first \"no.\"\n\n1. **Is the client population primarily browsers or mobile apps on variable networks?** If yes, HTTP/3 at the edge is worth enabling. If your traffic is service-to-service inside one cloud region, skip to step 5.\n2. **Does your edge (CDN, load balancer) support HTTP/3 termination with automatic fallback?** If it does, enable it there and stop. Do not push QUIC to the origin unless you have a measured reason.\n3. **Do you have observability that distinguishes HTTP/3 from HTTP/2 sessions?** If not, fix that first. Enabling a transport you cannot see is how you get a fallback bug you cannot diagnose.\n4. **Is there a measured client-side latency problem on lossy networks that HTTP/2 has not solved?** If yes, enabling at the edge is the cheap experiment. Measure time-to-first-byte and connection error rate by protocol version, not aggregate.\n5. **For origin-side QUIC: do you control both ends and is the path lossy or mobile?** If not, the cost/benefit rarely clears. Origin-side QUIC is a specialist move for specific topologies, not a default.\n\nFor most freelance and small-team backends serving Brazilian, Colombian, or Mexican clients, the answer lands at step 2: turn it on at the CDN, keep HTTP/2 at the origin, and spend the saved effort on the things that actually break — connection pooling, timeouts, and retry behavior.\n\n## Implementation details that bite\n\n### Alt-Svc and fallback\n\nThe server advertises HTTP/3 with a response header like:\n\n```http\nAlt-Svc: h3=\":443\"; ma=86400\n```\n\n`ma` is the max-age in seconds the client may remember the advertisement. If you serve this header and then break your UDP listener, clients will keep trying HTTP/3 for up to `ma` seconds before falling back. A short `ma` during rollout is a cheap safety valve.\n\n### Health checks must not assume UDP\n\nMost load balancer health checks are TCP or HTTP over TCP. If you add a QUIC listener, the health check still passes while UDP/443 is silently blocked upstream. You need a separate check — or at minimum an alert on the ratio of HTTP/3 to HTTP/2 sessions dropping to zero. A QUIC listener that accepts nothing looks healthy to a TCP check.\n\n### Timeouts and retries\n\nQUIC has its own idle timeout and its own loss detection. If your application layer also has aggressive timeouts, you can get double-timeout behavior: the transport retransmits while the app has already given up, or the app retries a request the transport was still delivering. Keep application-level timeouts longer than the transport's, and make retries idempotent. This is not new advice, but HTTP/3 makes the transport timing less visible because it is not in the kernel.\n\n### Observability\n\nQUIC exposes `qlog`, a structured JSON event log for connection-level events. It is the equivalent of a packet capture but at the protocol layer, and it is the tool to reach for when a connection behaves strangely. On the server side, make sure your access logs record the negotiated protocol version per request — many default log formats do not, which makes it impossible to answer \"did this user get HTTP/3 or fall back?\" after the fact.\n\n### The middlebox problem in reverse\n\nBecause QUIC encrypts transport headers, some enterprise networks treat it as unknown traffic and block it. If a meaningful share of your users are behind such networks, your HTTP/3 adoption rate will plateau and your HTTP/2 fallback path must be as fast as it was before. Do not let the fallback path rot.\n\n## What outcomes to expect, and their limits\n\nBe careful with numbers here. Published results vary by network conditions, and a figure measured on a simulated lossy link does not transfer to a well-provisioned datacenter path. What can be said with confidence:\n\n- The largest gains are on high-latency, lossy, mobile client networks, and they come mostly from fewer round trips at connection setup and per-stream loss isolation.\n- On stable, low-loss paths, the gain is small and may be negative once you account for userspace crypto CPU.\n- The benefit is front-loaded: the first connection and the first few hundred milliseconds benefit most. Long-lived connections amortize the handshake savings away.\n\nIllustrative arithmetic, not a measurement: assume a client on a network with 100 ms round-trip time. A TCP+TLS 1.3 first connection needs roughly three round trips before application data (TCP handshake plus TLS handshake). QUIC's combined handshake is roughly one. That is a difference of about 200 ms on the first byte, before any loss. On a 10 ms datacenter path the same arithmetic gives about 20 ms — real, but rarely the thing that determines whether a page feels fast. The point is the shape of the curve, not the specific milliseconds.\n\n## What to watch out for\n\n- **Disabling HTTP/2 \"because we have HTTP/3.\"** Never. Fallback is mandatory.\n- **Assuming the origin benefits.** If the edge terminates QUIC, the origin's connection pool behavior is unchanged. If you had head-of-line blocking problems at the origin, they are still there.\n- **Health checks that pass while UDP is blocked.** Add a protocol-aware check or an alert on session ratio.\n- **Logging without protocol version.** You cannot debug what you cannot see.\n- **CPU surprises at the edge.** Userspace crypto costs more per byte than kernel TCP with offload. Size accordingly.\n- **Treating 0-RTT as free.** 0-RTT resumption has replay considerations; do not use it for non-idempotent requests without understanding the tradeoff.\n\n## The broader lesson\n\nTransport upgrades change the shape of latency, not the shape of your application. The failure modes that hurt backend teams — unbounded connection pools, retries without idempotency, timeouts that fight each other, health checks that test the wrong thing — live above the transport and survive every protocol migration. HTTP/3 is worth enabling where it is cheap and observable, and it is not worth restructuring an origin for without a measured reason. The teams that get value from it treat it as one more edge setting with a fallback path, not as a platform migration.\n\n## How to apply this to your situation\n\nIn the next 30 minutes: open your edge or load balancer configuration and check two things. First, whether HTTP/3 is enabled with HTTP/2 still available as fallback — if HTTP/2 is disabled, re-enable it now. Second, whether your access logs record the negotiated protocol version per request. If they do not, add that field to the log format before you change anything else. You cannot evaluate an HTTP/3 rollout you cannot measure, and the fallback path is the part that breaks silently.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Does HTTP/3 improve origin-to-origin service calls?**\nRarely enough to justify the change. On a stable, low-loss path inside a cloud region, the handshake savings are amortized by connection pooling and the userspace crypto cost is paid on every byte. QUIC's advantages are largest on lossy, high-latency, mobile client networks. For service mesh traffic on a healthy network, the benefit is marginal.\n\n**What happens if a client's network blocks UDP/443?**\nThe client should fall back to HTTP/2 over TCP, which is why keeping the HTTP/2 listener is mandatory. Fallback is usually fast but not guaranteed to be instant, and it depends on the client honoring the `Alt-Svc` advertisement and its max-age. If you disabled HTTP/2, those users are broken outright, not degraded.\n\n**Do I need to change my application code to support HTTP/3?**\nIf the edge terminates QUIC and forwards HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 to your origin, no. If you terminate QUIC at your own load balancer or reverse proxy, the application still typically sees ordinary HTTP requests; the changes are in the proxy configuration, health checks, logging, and timeout tuning. Application-level code changes are rare.\n\n**Is HTTP/3 more expensive to run at the edge?**\nGenerally yes, per byte, because encryption and congestion control move into userspace rather than the kernel TCP stack with hardware offload. The cost is real but manageable at typical edge volumes; it becomes a sizing problem on high-throughput termination points. Measure CPU per request before and after enabling it.\n\n## Further reading\n\nThe QUIC and HTTP/3 specifications are the authoritative source: RFC 9000 (QUIC transport), RFC 9001 (TLS in QUIC), RFC 9114 (HTTP/3), and RFC 9002 (loss detection and congestion control). For operational debugging, the `qlog` schema documentation and your load balancer's HTTP/3 configuration reference are the two documents worth bookmarking before a rollout.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
